<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomPermission xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, DELIBERATELY — see the identical
        255-character-cap workaround note in Acquisition_Deal_Actions.customPermission-meta.xml
        and Disposition_Deal_Actions.customPermission-meta.xml.

        PHASE 1 (2026-08-12) of retiring the driver-flag concept — see
        agent-output/design-requirements-custom-permissions.md section 3.1. 🔴 USER DECISION
        OVERRIDING THAT DESIGN DOC'S RECOMMENDATION: the design doc recommended DEFERRING a
        Transaction custom permission because Transaction's gate has no FLS in it at all — it is
        already MEMBERSHIP-ONLY (Transaction_Stage_Actions_Access, layer 4, no companion
        User.Transaction_Driver__c field, no layer-5 authorization-flag set — see that file's own
        comment, which explicitly rejects building one). The user chose to build all three
        custom permissions in the same pass instead of waiting for "the first time someone wants
        the Transaction Advance Stage button hidden declaratively" (the design doc's named
        trigger). This file exists to make that future one-line change already possible.

        UPDATED — a later pass wired this permission into live code:
        TransactionActionPermissionService now calls
        FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Transaction_Stage_Actions'), so the "not yet consumed
        anywhere" claim below no longer holds; it is left in place, superseded, so this comment's
        history reads in order. No flexipage visibility rule references it (there was none to
        repoint: Transaction__c appears in zero of the seven driver-gated flexipages, because a
        visibility rule cannot test permission set membership, only $Permission). Granting it here,
        through the same membership-only capability set, is still population-preserving in exactly
        the same way as Transaction_Stage_Actions_Access's own classAccesses grant is
        redundant-but-self-documenting against DPEG_Apex_Access — see that file's comment.

        Granted through Transaction_Stage_Actions_Access.permissionset-meta.xml, a LAYER-4
        capability set, member of the DPEG_Transaction_Team permission set group — NOT a layer-5
        authorization-flag set. That is a deliberate, correct exception to the pattern the other
        two custom permissions in this repo follow: Transaction was ALREADY membership-only before
        this change (the user's own prior decision, recorded in that file's header), and adding a
        custom permission here does not introduce a second factor — it just gives the existing
        one-factor gate a token that can, in future, also drive a declarative visibility rule.
        🔴 DO NOT add a User.Transaction_Driver__c field or a Transaction_Deal_Driver
        layer-5 set on the strength of this file's existence — that would reintroduce the
        two-factor model the user explicitly rejected for this module.
    -->
    <label>Transaction Stage Actions</label>
    <description>Authorization token for the Transaction stage-advance action, read via FeatureManagement.checkPermission by TransactionActionPermissionService. Granted membership-only via Transaction_Stage_Actions_Access.</description>
    <isLicensed>false</isLicensed>
</CustomPermission>
